1
00:00:00,555 --> 00:00:05,626
(Q11&12（多选）) Today we're pleased to have on the show Alice Bussell from the Dolphin Conservation Trust.

2
00:00:05,626 --> 00:00:07,599
Tell us about the Trust, Alice.&nbsp;

3
00:00:07,599 --> 00:00:13,593
Well, obviously its purpose is to protect dolphins in seas all around the world.

4
00:00:13,593 --> 00:00:20,954
It tries to raise people's awareness of the problems these marine creatures are suffering because of pollution and other threats.

5
00:00:20,954 --> 00:00:31,620
It started ten years ago and it's one of the fastest growing animal charities in the country - although it's still fairly small compared with the big players in animal protection.

6
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:46,335
[A：定位句： children] We are particularly proud of the work we do in education,last year we visited a huge number of schools in different parts of the country, going round to talk to children and young people aged from five to eighteen.

7
00:00:46,335 --> 00:00:50,718
[A选项排除句] In fact, about thirty-five per cent of our members are children.

8
00:00:50,718 --> 00:00:58,668
[选项C定位句：campaign + Q11答案句（C） ✅] The charity uses its money to support campaigns -for example, for changes in fishing policy and so forth.

9
00:00:58,668 --> 00:01:06,465
[选项D定位句+D排除❌： hope soon to be ... , 题目 employs 时态不一致] It hopes soon to be able to employ its first full-time biologist - with dolphin expertise - to monitor populations.

10
00:01:06,465 --> 00:01:16,951
[E定位句： volunteer， Q12答案句✅：many people give ... on voluntary basis ... = volunteer in various way] Of course, many people give their services on a voluntary basis and we now have volunteers working in observation, office work and other things.

11
00:01:16,951 --> 00:01:23,948
(Q13（单选）) I should also tell you about the award we won from the Charity Commission last year - for our work in education.

12
00:01:23,948 --> 00:01:31,417
[Q13答案句（B）+ 干扰项A] Although it's not meant an enormous amount of money for us, it has made our activities even more widely publicised and understood.

13
00:01:31,417 --> 00:01:37,629
[Q13干扰项C] In the long term it may not bring in extra members but we're hoping it'll have this effect.

14
00:01:37,629 --> 00:01:41,272
(Q14) Is it possible to see dolphins in UK waters?

15
00:01:41,272 --> 00:01:42,156
Yes.

16
00:01:42,156 --> 00:01:43,890
In several locations.

17
00:01:43,890 --> 00:01:47,421
And we have a big project in the east part of Scotland.

18
00:01:47,421 --> 00:01:51,647
[Q14干扰项C， 提到shipping， 但 little（排除方法： 否定）] This has long been a haven for dolphins because it has very little shipping.

19
00:01:51,647 --> 00:01:58,400
However, that may be about to change soon because oil companies want to increase exploration there.

20
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:07,072
[Q14干扰项B（pollution= oil leaks， 但little）+Q14答案句A（noise，听出机会 although）] We're campaigning against this because, although there'll be little pollution from oil, exploration creates a lot of underwater noise.

21
00:02:07,072 --> 00:02:10,711
It means the dolphins can't rest and socialise.

22
00:02:10,711 --> 00:02:15,316
(Q15) This is how I became interested in dolphin conservation in the first place.

23
00:02:15,316 --> 00:02:20,090
[Q15干扰项A， never seen one 排除方式： 否定] I had never seen one and I hadn't been particularly interested in them at school.

24
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:31,379
[Q15答案句C： came across a story = book about them] Then I came across this story about a family of dolphins who had to leave their home in the Moray Firth because of the oil companies and about a child who campaigned to save them.

25
00:02:31,379 --> 00:02:35,532
I couldn't put the book down - I was hooked.

26
00:02:35,532 --> 00:02:40,145
(Q16（Matching）) I'm sure our listeners will want to find out what they can do to help.

27
00:02:40,145 --> 00:02:43,118
You mentioned the 'Adopt a Dolphin' scheme.

28
00:02:43,118 --> 00:02:44,760
Can you tell us about that?

29
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:45,683
Of course!

30
00:02:45,683 --> 00:02:49,040
People can choose one of our dolphins to sponsor.

31
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,507
They receive a picture of it and news updates.

32
00:02:52,507 --> 00:03:01,681
I'd like to tell you about four which are currently being adopted by our members: Moondancer, Echo, Kiwi and Samson.

33
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:11,578
[Q16答案句] Unfortunately, Echo is being rather elusive this year and hasn't yet been sighted by our observers but we remain optimistic that he'll be out there soon.

34
00:03:11,578 --> 00:03:26,075
(Q17) [Q17答案句] All the others have been out in force - Samson and Moondancer are often photographed together but it is Kiwi who's our real character as she seems to love coming up close for the cameras and we've captured her on film hundreds of times.

35
00:03:26,075 --> 00:03:39,138
(Q18) [Q18答案句（找energetic的）] They all have their own personalities - Moondancer is very elegant and curves out and into the water very smoothly, whereas Samson has a lot of energy - he's always leaping out of the water with great vigour.

36
00:03:39,138 --> 00:03:48,909
(Q19) [Q19答案句（newest）] You'd probably expect him to be the youngest - he's not quite - that's Kiwi - but Samson's the latest of our dolphins to be chosen for the scheme.

37
00:03:48,909 --> 00:03:53,492
(Q20) Kiwi makes a lot of noise so we can often pick her out straightaway.&nbsp;

38
00:03:53,492 --> 00:04:02,866
[Q20答案句（has a particularly large fin  = unusual shape 解释型）] Echo and Moondancer are noisy too, but Moondancer's easy to find because she has a particularly large fin on her back, which makes her easy to identify.&nbsp;

39
00:04:02,866 --> 00:04:05,282
So, yes, they're all very different...

40
00:04:05,282 --> 00:04:07,782
Well, they sound a fascinating group ...

